<title>ITALY: FORECAST - Lira seen in ERM before end-Nov at 1,000.</title>
<headline>FORECAST - Lira seen in ERM before end-Nov at 1,000.</headline>
<dateline>MILAN 1996-11-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>The lira is widely expected to return to Europe's exchange rate mechanism (ERM) before the end of this month with many in the market forecasting its central parity against the mark will be 1,000 lire, a survey showed.</p>
<p>A Reuter poll of 10 economists in Italy and London showed half of those asked expected the Bank of Italy to cut its key discount rate once the lira had returned to the grid.</p>
<p>Following are the results of the survey which was carried out late on Wednesday:</p>
<p>1 - When do you expect the lira to re-enter the ERM?</p>
<p>Weekend of:</p>
<p>    November 23			  3</p>
<p>    November 30			  2</p>
<p>    December  7			  1</p>
<p>    December 14 (Dublin summit)    2</p>
<p>    Before year end		    1</p>
<p>    Not before year end		1</p>
<p>2 - At what level do you expect the lira's central parity against the mark to be set?</p>
<p>    Firmer than 1,000		  0</p>
<p>    1,000				  6</p>
<p>    1,000-1,010			  2</p>
<p>    1,010-1,020			  1</p>
<p>    1,020-1,030			  1</p>
<p>3 - Do you expect a discount rate cut before or after ERM entry?</p>
<p>    Before				 4</p>
<p>    After				  5</p>
<p>    Before year end		    1</p>
<p>If so, by how much?</p>
<p>    25 basis points		    1</p>
<p>    50 basis points		    5</p>
<p>    75 basis points		    2</p>
<p>    50-75				  1</p>
<p>    Don't know			   1</p>
<p>Economists from the following banks participated: San Paolo, HSBC, Paribas, UBS, Banco di Sardegna, NatWest, BCI, Morgan Stanley, Nomura and one other who declined to be named.</p>
<p>--Milan bureau +392 66129507</p>
</text>
